note: >-
  One T1 change, two T2 changes, three T3 docs. The T1 change is
  docs/design-flow.html — the close sequence's new step ① — and it is what the human Reviewed-By
  trailer on this commit signs. That is stated first because it is the change a reader must not
  miss - a design WU that amends the process every future design run follows.

  T2 is two records and no contract. docs/decisions/ gains DEC-38, the incident behind step ①,
  and decisions-index.html registers it. What T2 does NOT gain is a use-case or rulebook edit,
  and that silence is the correct outcome rather than an omission - a design run decides how a
  screen will look and changes no user-observable contract, so UC-4's surface gains the export,
  import and clear controls when W6 BUILDS them; stating them now would put a capability in the
  use-case KB that the app does not have. The rulebook is untouched for the same reason plus a
  specific one - the only T2 write-back this line of work owed was run 10's Lexicon question, and
  W5 already landed it, so `export` has its row and this run consumes it (receipt verdict -
  inherited) rather than proposing a term.

  The run's own four proposals are NOT T2 edits this WU may make, and none was made - Dialog
  (promote ModalShell) and Menu belong to the collection, safe-answer-takes-focus belongs to the
  rulebook, and the ⋮ glyph entry belongs to IconButton's TSDoc. A run proposes and an owner
  performs. Two of the four moved during the rework - the glyph entries went from three faces to
  one, because with the acts collapsed into a menu they are words and no face is spent on any of
  them; and Menu was added, because Select disqualifies itself twice in its own @avoid and the
  collection has no action menu at 28 entries. Two proposed components is a lot of proposed
  surface for one design, and Proposals says so explicitly - W6 may build both local and mint
  neither, the way this run's ask is local.

  The close-sequence step is the one place where this WU performed rather than proposed, and the
  distinction is deliberate. A vocabulary or component gap is a proposal because an owner exists
  who must decide it. A defect in the flow's own procedure has no such owner short of the flow
  doc itself, and documentation-system.html's checklist routes a changed process to its T1 doc in
  the same session - while a T3 run is archived and never re-read, so leaving it as a proposal
  would have lost it.

  One item is deliberately NOT distilled and is recorded as owed instead - tauri.conf.json's
  minWidth must rise from 800 to 850 for the header to hold one line at every reachable width.
  The window config is not a design artifact and not a doc, so it sits in the run's Blocked-on
  with W6 named as owner, beside the cross-year timestamp overflow, rather than being quietly
  changed here.

  One fact per tier held deliberately - the endpoint shapes live in src/api/routes.rs and are
  named in the run only by what the screen needs from them; the component contracts live in each
  component's TSDoc and the run cites @use/@avoid rather than restating states; the run's
  own account of which capability is used or deliberately unused lives in its capabilities table
  rather than being repeated in the Frame, which is why the Frame's Given line is a terse
  ·-separated list; and DEC-38 holds the incident so design-flow.html can hold the mechanism
  alone.

  Every doc was snapshotted before its first edit this session and DEC-38 at creation, so
  <doc>.html?diff=latest shows exactly what changed. verify-doc over all 59 governed docs in the
  tree - none red; the run itself green as design-run at 17/17 soft, 13/13 hard, 0 suppressions,
  which required reopening it for the fix and re-archiving it at the close so the checks actually
  ran. `ui specimens --check` renders it at 7 mounts and 15 buttons. design-flow.html and both
  decisions docs are ungoverned by verify-doc - it reports them ungoverned rather than green - so
  what was checked on them instead is that every relative href resolves on disk and that each
  renders in headless Chrome with no page error.

  A standing limit worth recording, because this WU is its evidence - neither gate reads what
  these rounds found. verify-doc reads structure and never prose or pixels; the render gate fails
  only an EMPTY mount. A mockup that renders in the right place in the wrong colour, at the wrong
  width, or differently tomorrow than today is green by both, and a four-cell row under two
  headers is green by both. Rounds 1-11 read this document; rounds 12-14 rendered it, and
  everything they found was invisible to every check.
